---
/**
 * Affiliate disclosure. Truthful in both states: while `AFFILIATE_ENABLED` is
 * false the page says plainly that the site carries no affiliate links, and
 * describes the policy that applies if that changes. Flipping the flag in
 * src/lib/affiliate.ts switches it to the present tense.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { affiliateLive } from '../lib/affiliate';

const live = affiliateLive();

const description = live
  ? 'How ToolFaultFinder uses Amazon affiliate links, and the rules we hold ourselves to.'
  : 'ToolFaultFinder currently carries no affiliate links. What would change if that ever does.';
---

<Base title="Affiliate Disclosure" description={description}>
  <div class="wrap page-body">
    <p class="kicker" style="margin-top: 2rem;">The money bit</p>
    <h1>Affiliate Disclosure</h1>

    {live ? (
      <>
        <p>
          <strong>ToolFaultFinder uses Amazon affiliate links.</strong> Some links to parts
          and consumables on this site are tagged with our Amazon Associates tracking ID. If
          you buy something after following one, Amazon pays us a small commission. It costs
          you nothing extra — the price is the same as it would be without the link.
        </p>
        <p>
          As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying purchases.
        </p>
        <h2>If you're not in the UK</h2>
        <p>
          Our links go to <strong>Amazon UK</strong>, because this is a UK site — the prices
          we quote are in pounds and the parts we name are the ones sold here. About half our
          readers are not in the UK, and Australia is our second-largest country, so to be
          plain about it: many parts on Amazon UK do ship internationally, but some won't, and
          you may well do better searching your own local Amazon for the part name we give.
          The part names and any manufacturer numbers are the useful bit — the link is just a
          convenience.
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        <p>
          <strong>ToolFaultFinder currently carries no affiliate links, adverts, or
          sponsorships.</strong> Nothing on this site earns money if you click it, and no
          page has been written with a commission in mind. Where we name a part you might
          need, it is named because you need it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That may change — affiliate links on the parts we already name are the most likely
          way this site would eventually pay for itself. If it does, this page will say so
          plainly, every affiliate link will be marked where it appears, and the rules below
          are what we would hold ourselves to.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>What {live ? 'does not' : 'would not'} change</h2>
    <p>
      The verdict comes first and the parts list comes after it. Every entry on this site
      answers <em>“is it worth fixing?”</em> honestly, including the many cases where the
      answer is that the fix is free, or that the tool is not worth the parts. Those verdicts
      are written from the sources, before anyone looks at whether a part is purchasable, and
      they {live ? 'are not' : 'would not be'} rewritten to sell anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice that means a lot of pages here {live ? 'have' : 'would have'} no parts list
      at all. A grinder that needs a £6 pair of brushes gets a parts list; a jigsaw cutting
      at an angle because it is being pushed too hard does not, because nothing needs buying.
      A site that always finds something for you to buy is a shopping site wearing a repair
      site's clothes, and it would be obvious.
    </p>

    <h2>Part numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Where we give a manufacturer's part number it comes from a real document — a manual, a
      parts diagram, or an official support page — not from a shopping listing. We leave the
      number out rather than guess it, because a wrong part number costs you money. Check any
      part against your own tool's model and serial before ordering; ranges get revised, and
      the same model name can cover more than one part.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      If anything here looks like it is pointing you at a purchase you do not need, we would
      genuinely rather know. <a href="/contact/">Tell us</a>, or leave a comment on the entry
      itself — corrections are published.
    </p>

    <p style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <a href="/how-we-research/">How we research these entries</a>
      &ensp;·&ensp;
      <a href="/terms/">Terms &amp; Disclaimer</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
